# Copy to `.env.local` and fill in. Committed on purpose — it carries key names, no
# values. Everything here comes from the Cognito pool, the RDS instance and the S3
# bucket, and none of it is a secret except DATABASE_URL and the S3 access key.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- browser
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000
NEXT_PUBLIC_COGNITO_REGION=
NEXT_PUBLIC_COGNITO_USER_POOL_ID=
NEXT_PUBLIC_COGNITO_CLIENT_ID=
NEXT_PUBLIC_COGNITO_DOMAIN=
NEXT_PUBLIC_COGNITO_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:3000/api/auth/callback/cognito

# -------------------------------------------------------------------- server
COGNITO_REGION=
COGNITO_USER_POOL_ID=
COGNITO_CLIENT_ID=
COGNITO_ISSUER=

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------- ai
# Microsoft Foundry (Azure) — the AI provider this build is wired to. The endpoint and
# the deployment name are configuration; the key is a real secret and belongs in
# `.env.local` (gitignored), never in this template.
#
# `scripts/foundry-setup.sh` provisions the resource and the deployment with the Azure
# CLI and writes every value below. The deployment is what the app sends as the model:
# Foundry addresses a model by the name you gave its deployment, not by the upstream
# model id, so `AZURE_OPENAI_DEPLOYMENT` must match the deployment name.
#
# The deployment is a vision model: it reads the photograph, and everything with a rupee
# sign on it is still computed server-side. The Hindi voice note does not go to it — a
# vision deployment cannot read audio, and this subscription hosts no audio-capable
# model — so it is transcribed first (below) and the verbatim transcript is handed to the
# model as the collector's own words. Two calls, one answer.
AZURE_OPENAI_ENDPOINT=
AZURE_OPENAI_API_KEY=
AZURE_OPENAI_DEPLOYMENT=gpt-4.1-mini
# Azure Speech, for the Hindi voice note. The region must be the AI Services account's
# region (`<region>.stt.speech.microsoft.com`); the key defaults to AZURE_OPENAI_API_KEY,
# because an AI Services account's Speech key is the account key. Set AZURE_SPEECH_KEY
# only when a separate Speech resource is used.
AZURE_SPEECH_REGION=
AZURE_SPEECH_KEY=

# --------------------------------------------------------------- database
# AWS RDS for PostgreSQL (ecoepr-db, ap-south-1). The password belongs in Secrets
# Manager or SSM, never in this file.
#
# sslmode=no-verify because node-postgres reads `require` as verify-full, which fails
# against the RDS certificate chain. RDS has rds.force_ssl=1, so plaintext is refused.
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://ecoepr_admin:<password>@ecoepr-db.<id>.ap-south-1.rds.amazonaws.com:5432/ecoepr?sslmode=no-verify
DATABASE_URL=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------ s3
# The media archive — where the photo or voice note behind a valuation is kept.
#
# Bucket and region are configuration, the access key is a real secret, and they
# belong in `.env.local` (gitignored). The bucket is private: public access is blocked
# at the bucket and objects are AES-256 encrypted by bucket default.
#
# The key names are `S3_*` and not the standard `AWS_*` pair on purpose. Amplify
# Hosting refuses to store any environment variable beginning with `AWS` — "Environment
# variables cannot start with the reserved prefix \"AWS\"" — so a deployment cannot be
# given `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID` at all. `lib/s3/archive.ts` therefore reads these two names
# directly and falls back to the SDK's own chain, which is what covers a shared profile
# locally and an instance role on compute that has one. Neither line is required: with
# no key anywhere, the archive reports itself as off and the routes still price.
#
# The policy is deliberately narrow: `media/*` in this one bucket, nothing wider. A
# different S3_PREFIX needs the policy changed with it.
#
# Leave S3_BUCKET empty and the archive is simply off: the AI routes still analyse and
# price a submission, and say in the response that nothing was stored.
S3_BUCKET=
S3_REGION=
S3_PREFIX=media
S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
